  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ab1e71a2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### To setup everytime\n",
    "We're going to be using a number of tools to help us:\n",
    "* uproot: lets us read .root files typically used in particle physics into data formats used in Python\n",
    "* numpy: provides numerical calculations such as histogramming\n",
    "* matplotlib: common tool for making plots, figures, images, visualisations\n",
    "* awkward: a python package for dealing conveniently with lists that are different lengths\n",
    "* vector: a python package for making and manipulating vectors\n",
    "\n",
    "In case you see an error when importing one of these packages, try restarting the kernel (the \"session\" in Colab) to make sure the packages that were installed in the last cell have been properly loaded."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "33adba12",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np # for numerical calculations such as histogramming\n",
    "import uproot # to read .root files as dataframes\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt # for plotting\n",
    "from matplotlib.ticker import MaxNLocator,AutoMinorLocator # for minor ticks\n",
    "import awkward as ak # for handling complex and nested data structures efficiently\n",
    "import vector # For convenient 4-vector manipulation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eea9ffd0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Top-Antitop Production\n",
    "To observe non-prompt lepton phenomena, we'll start with a common and easily identifiable case. \n",
    "\n",
    "<div align=\"center\"><img src=\"Https://github.com/atlas-outreach-data-tools/notebooks-collection-opendata/blob/master/13-TeV-examples/uproot_python/images/feynman_diagrams/ttbar_decay_allproducts.png?raw=true\" style=\"width:40%\"></div>\n",
    "\n",
    "Top-Antitop Production (commonly referred to as ttbar production) is dominantly produced via gluon fusion during the collision of protons at high energies, like at the LHC. Drawn above is a [Feynman Diagram](https://cds.cern.ch/record/2759490/files/Feynman%20Diagrams%20-%20ATLAS%20Cheat%20Sheet.pdf) of the possible decay channels for ttbar production. As is shown, both top quarks decay into oppositely charged W bosons, as well as a bottom or antibottom quark. W bosons can decay into either quarks, or less commonly, a lepton-neutrino pair, creating our prompt leptons. However, the bottom/antibottom quark pair will also hadronize and create b-jets, which can create semileptonically decaying hadrons, generating extra leptons. \n",
    "\n",
    "Generally, we account for the leptonic decay of the W bosons as this is considered part of the initial decay, however these \"extra leptons\" which appear from hadronic decay in a smaller percentage of cases are considered non-prompt, and must be removed. In this analysis, we'll focus on lepton-inclusive decay channels, where every event will produce at least one prompt lepton, and there is no chance of decay only into hadrons (quarks). \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "41f967ca",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Let's Get Some Data!\n",
    "We're going to use the `atlasopenmagic` package to acquire and cache our data. This is a custom package designed by ATLAS Open Data to allow easy access to ATLAS public datasets. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b65c76de",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Let's start by importing atlasopenmagic so we can search for our data.\n",
    "import atlasopenmagic as atom\n",
    "# We're gonna set our release to a specific version, as there's a good example dataset in this one.\n",
    "atom.set_release('2025e-13tev-beta')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c0ba17fe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finding Data\n",
    "Let's start by finding some data that would most likely have some non-prompt leptons mixed in."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "fa765090",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# We can start by searching in the metadata for data surrounding ttbar production, our most common non-prompt lepton event.\n",
    "atom.match_metadata(field='keywords',value='ttbar')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5ee30bf5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "That's a good amount of options! However, we also want to make sure our data contains prompt leptons as well. For this, we'll use the dataset labeled \"nonallhad\", meaning that at least one of the W bosons decays non-hadronically. This sample is filtered so that at least one of the produced W-bosons decays leptonically rather than fully-hadronically (non-allhad), acting as a source of prompt leptons for our events. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "ef287a49",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Let's load our data. We set cache to false so we only grab events that we request, instead of loading the entire 16 GB dataset.\n",
    "files_list = atom.get_urls('PhPy8EG_A14_ttbar_hdamp258p75_nonallhad', protocol='root', cache=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Here we'll build a tree using the files we just pulled, using uproot to view the root files.\n",
    "ttbar_tree = uproot.open(files_list[0])[\"analysis\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "# And we'll print the amount of events available to choose from\n",
    "print(f\"Events available: {ttbar_tree.num_entries:,}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "071ba966",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "That's more than enough events to visualize the phenomenon we're looking for. Let's grab 5,000,000 of these events, and require at least 20.0 GeV of transverse momentum, so we primarily see high-energy leptons that could have been produced by W boson decay, the prompt event we're looking for. \n",
    "\n",
    "### What should we expect?\n",
    "\n",
    "If our sample only contains prompt leptons, then what we should see is a distribution of most events with either 0 or 1 lepton and a smaller few with 2 leptons. This is because both W bosons decaying leptonically is quite rare, with a 22% chance of any W boson decaying leptonically, along with a small contribution from Tau decays to electrons/muons. Due to our sample requiring at least 1 leptonic decay, this chance will be less relevant in our analysis. However, as neutrinos and tau are one of the possible results from the event, we will still most likely see less dilepton events. We should also see decidedly 0 events with more than 2 leptons, as this should be impossible if we are only seeing prompt leptons. As prompt leptons can only come from the two W bosons in the ttbar events we are looking at, events with more than 2 leptons indicate the presence of non-prompt leptons in our data.     "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "799654da",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "pt_cut = 20.0  # GeV\n",
    "max_events = 1_000_000  # Change to None to run over all events\n",
    "\n",
    "# We'll load the event counts without loading the whole ROOT file into memory.\n",
    "multiplicity_counts = np.zeros(1, dtype=np.int64)\n",
    "events_processed = 0\n",
    "\n",
    "# We'll define what variable we're searching in, our step size, our limit, and what library we'll be using.\n",
    "for batch in ttbar_tree.iterate(\n",
    "    expressions=[\"lep_pt\"],\n",
    "    entry_stop=max_events,\n",
    "    step_size=100_000,\n",
    "    library=\"ak\",\n",
    "):\n",
    "    # lep_pt is jagged: each event can contain a different number of leptons.\n",
    "    n_leptons = ak.to_numpy(ak.sum(batch[\"lep_pt\"] > pt_cut, axis=1))\n",
    "    batch_counts = np.bincount(n_leptons)\n",
    "\n",
    "    if len(batch_counts) > len(multiplicity_counts):\n",
    "        multiplicity_counts = np.pad(\n",
    "            multiplicity_counts, (0, len(batch_counts) - len(multiplicity_counts))\n",
    "        )\n",
    "    multiplicity_counts[:len(batch_counts)] += batch_counts\n",
    "    events_processed += len(n_leptons)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Processed {events_processed:,} events.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2177900f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Plotting\n",
    "Now that the data is processed for us, we'll plot it and look at what we actually see in the data. The below code will generate a bar graph of lepton counts in reconstructed events."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 20,
   "id": "e3352a3c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 800x500 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "n_values = np.arange(len(multiplicity_counts))\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "\n",
    "bars = ax.bar(\n",
    "    n_values,\n",
    "    multiplicity_counts,\n",
    "    width=0.75,\n",
    "    color=\"#15A1FF\",\n",
    "    edgecolor=\"black\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(r\"Number of reconstructed leptons with $p_T > 20$ GeV\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Number of events\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(r\"Lepton multiplicity in $t\\bar{t}$ non-all-hadronic events\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Display the event counts on a logarithmic scale.\n",
    "ax.set_yscale(\"log\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylim(bottom=0.8,top=max(multiplicity_counts) * 10)\n",
    "ax.xaxis.set_major_locator(MaxNLocator(integer=True))\n",
    "ax.grid(axis=\"y\",which=\"both\",alpha=0.25,)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Label non-empty bars with their event counts.\n",
    "ax.bar_label(\n",
    "    bars,\n",
    "    labels=[\n",
    "        f\"{count:,}\" if count > 0 else \"\"\n",
    "        for count in multiplicity_counts\n",
    "    ],\n",
    "    padding=3,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.text(\n",
    "    0.70,\n",
    "    0.90,\n",
    "    \"ATLAS Open Data\",\n",
    "    transform=ax.transAxes,\n",
    "    fontsize=13,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.text(\n",
    "    0.70,\n",
    "    0.84,\n",
    "    \"for education\",\n",
    "    transform=ax.transAxes,\n",
    "    fontsize=11,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ebdb3a16",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "If we follow the traditional assumption, this is not quite what we should be expecting. As mentioned before, the presence of 3 or 4 lepton events in this analysis should be impossible if we are focusing on prompt leptons alone. What we see differs from this, as we have a significant contribution from the 0- and 1-lepton bin, as well as a smaller contribution from the 2-lepton bin, followed by small but present 3- and 4-lepton bins. By this idea, we have direct evidence to support a contribution from non-prompt leptons! However, this is nothing to celebrate over, as this could invalidate a formal analysis. \n",
    "\n",
    "### 0-Lepton Bin \n",
    "\n",
    "If the used sample requires at least one of the W-bosons to decay semi-leptonically, how are we still getting such a huge 0-lepton bin? This is an important aspect to explain, and would require significant attention in a full-scale analysis. The first, most obvious way that we get that vast 0-lepton bin is from Tau hadronic decay. The Tau lepton is a heavy standard model lepton with a very short lifetime. Because of this, Tau are never directly detected by ATLAS. Due to their high mass, they have the energy necessary to decay hadronically into light-flavor quarks, eliminating the leptonic contribution from a real lepton. This is the primary decay channel for the Tau, with around two-thirds of tau decaying hadronically. This, by effect, causes a significant amount of events appearing to produce 0 leptons to the detector, while a prompt lepton had truly existed during the decay. Along with the loose requirements for $|p_t|$ and $\\eta$, this can be used to help explain why the 0-lepton bin exists, and why it is so significant to this analysis. \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9f8e152a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Photon Conversion\n",
    "\n",
    "<div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; gap: 20px;\"><img src=\"https://github.com/atlas-outreach-data-tools/notebooks-collection-opendata/blob/master/13-TeV-examples/uproot_python/images/feynman_diagrams/H_toploop_yy_feyn.png?raw=true\" style=\"width: 40%;\"><img src=\"https://github.com/atlas-outreach-data-tools/notebooks-collection-opendata/blob/master/13-TeV-examples/uproot_python/images/feynman_diagrams/H_Wloop_yy_feyn.png?raw=true\" style=\"width: 40%;\">\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "In order to study the non-prompt leptons originating from detector material interactions, we can use one of the decay modes of the Higgs Boson. Used initially from 2012 to the modern day to identify Higgs in data, the Higgs diphoton decay channel is a rare decay mode that happens in about 0.2% of Higgs decays. The Higgs boson has a very short lifetime, and rapidly decays into one of many modes, in some rare cases decaying into two photons, which is most commonly mediated by a loop usually involving top quarks or W bosons (shown above). \n",
    "\n",
    "Sometimes, when one of these high energy photons passes by another electric field or atomic nucleus they will split into leptons. This is known as [Pair Production](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pair_production), and is a result of the high energy in these photons due to the decay of the 125 GeV Higgs boson. These leptons can then be picked up by the ATLAS detector, and result in unintended lepton signals. Interaction with the detector causing this phenomenon also makes this a dectector-material lepton effect, often occurring in the first-line pixel detector, allowing us to view how the detector's physical matter can act as a significant source of fake leptons in analysis. Here we use the $H\\to\\gamma\\gamma$ channel not because it is common, but because it is a cleaner single-parent energetic decay, allowing us to pick out and observe this phenomena easier."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1647ce9a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Getting H $\\rightarrow$ yy Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2611e1e7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "More often than not, we refer to the Higgs diphoton decay channel as simply \"Hyy\" or \"H->gamgam\" to save us some space in the database (\"yy\" because the greek symbol gamma, \"$\\gamma$\", looks like the letter y). Since we have a lot of data to choose from, let's search for datasets with \"Hyy\" in the title using the `match_metadata` command in `Atlasopenmagic`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2c48456b",
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": true
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Match metadata will search for all of the datasets which contain a string or value given.\n",
    "atom.match_metadata(field='physics_short',value='Hyy')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "44d67bd1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Looks like we have a good selection here. We'll go with the sample which includes only Hyy, without other decay channels included. Remember, we're only looking at non-prompt leptons from a Higgs boson decay to photons. Including other variables like W or Z boson decay could create extra leptons in our range of analysis, inflating the number of non-prompt events present. \n",
    "\n",
    "In this analysis, what we'll mainly be looking for is a signature from non-prompt leptons produced from pair production. We're going to compare the mass of events with two photons, and events with a photon and an electron. Since we're looking at Higgs boson decays, we'll most likely see a large jump in the amount of diphoton events that hover around 125 GeV in mass (the mass of the Higgs boson). If a significant amount of photons convert into electrons, then we'll also see a jump in the amount of photon-electron events at that same 125 GeV mass. It is important to note that via pair production, only electrons can be created, so we will only need to look at electrons as our non-prompt leptons for this sample."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "d5b420e3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# We'll define our files under a different variable, and use the files listed under our chosen dataset.\n",
    "hyy_files = atom.get_urls(\"aMCH7EG_VBF_HC_Hyy\",protocol=\"root\",cache=False)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e3ff0935",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Great! Now we'll run some code to load the variables that we need and define some basic functions. We have a function which creates four-vectors, a kind of vector which shows the momentum, direction, and energy, and a function which orders particles by highest transverse momentum."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "c5ae29b9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Only load the information needed for this demonstration.\n",
    "variables = [\n",
    "    \"photon_pt\",\n",
    "    \"photon_eta\",\n",
    "    \"photon_phi\",\n",
    "    \"photon_e\",\n",
    "    \"photon_isLooseID\",\n",
    "    \"lep_type\",\n",
    "    \"lep_pt\",\n",
    "    \"lep_eta\",\n",
    "    \"lep_phi\",\n",
    "    \"lep_e\",\n",
    "    \"lep_isLooseID\",\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define a function to generate four-vectors.\n",
    "def make_four_vectors(pt, eta, phi, energy):\n",
    "    return vector.zip({\n",
    "        \"pt\": pt,\n",
    "        \"eta\": eta,\n",
    "        \"phi\": phi,\n",
    "        \"e\": energy,\n",
    "    })\n",
    "\n",
    "# Place the highest transverse momentum first in the list of particles.\n",
    "def sort_by_pt(particles):\n",
    "    order = ak.argsort(\n",
    "        particles.pt,\n",
    "        axis=1,\n",
    "        ascending=False,\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    return particles[order]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b106872e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data Processing\n",
    "The following code is going to load our dataset in chunks of 10,000 events at a time. We'll construct [four-vectors](https://cds.cern.ch/record/2919871/files/4-vectors%20and%20invariant%20mass%20cheat%20sheet.pdf) for each photon and electron/positron, select good particles by applying a cut to keep particles with transverse momentum above 20 GeV, and calculate invariant mass. By splitting up events by particle type, we end up with two bins: one containing the ordered invariant mass of diphoton events, and one containing the ordered invariant mass of events with one photon and one electron. \n",
    "\n",
    "For this analysis, we group electrons and positrons together, as they are nearly identical to the detector, and their charge is stored in a separate variable which is of no need to this analysis. If you want to know more about 4-vectors and invariant mass, check out our other notebook, [Find the Z](https://opendata.atlas.cern/docs/13TeV25Doc/StandardModel), where we dive deeper into what these things are. The invariant mass of a system of particles is derived from the equation $m^2 = E^2 - |\\vec{p}|^2$ using [], built from the summed four-momenta. It is called \"invariant\" because it is the same across all speeds/reference frames. This means that a combines invariant mass of 125 GeV for a two particle system translates to \"these two objects came from something with a mass of 125 GeV.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Note: This cell may take some time to run, allow 1-2 minutes for processing."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1442a1fc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# We can first define empty lists where we'll place our values\n",
    "diphoton_mass_list = []\n",
    "electron_photon_mass_list = []\n",
    "\n",
    "# We'll process by recursively running through every file in the file list\n",
    "for file_name in hyy_files:\n",
    "    # Pull the files again using uproot\n",
    "    hyy_tree = uproot.open(file_name + \":analysis\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Define our step size, load the variables we need, and load our library\n",
    "    for data in hyy_tree.iterate(\n",
    "        variables,\n",
    "        step_size=10_000,\n",
    "        library=\"ak\",\n",
    "    ):\n",
    "        # We'll construct four vectors for photons using the variables we defined earlier\n",
    "        photons = make_four_vectors(\n",
    "            data[\"photon_pt\"],\n",
    "            data[\"photon_eta\"],\n",
    "            data[\"photon_phi\"],\n",
    "            data[\"photon_e\"],\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        # And then again for our electrons\n",
    "        electrons = make_four_vectors(\n",
    "            data[\"lep_pt\"],\n",
    "            data[\"lep_eta\"],\n",
    "            data[\"lep_phi\"],\n",
    "            data[\"lep_e\"],\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Photon selection\n",
    "        good_photon = (\n",
    "            (photons.pt > 20)\n",
    "            & (abs(photons.eta) < 2.37)\n",
    "            & data[\"photon_isLooseID\"]\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Electron selection\n",
    "        good_electron = (\n",
    "            (abs(data[\"lep_type\"]) == 11)\n",
    "            & (electrons.pt > 20)\n",
    "            & (abs(electrons.eta) < 2.47)\n",
    "            & data[\"lep_isLooseID\"]\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Sort photons by transverse momentum\n",
    "        photons = sort_by_pt(\n",
    "            photons[good_photon]\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Sort electrons by transverse momentum\n",
    "        electrons = sort_by_pt(\n",
    "            electrons[good_electron]\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        # H -> gamma gamma reconstruction\n",
    "        has_two_photons = (\n",
    "            ak.num(photons, axis=1) >= 2\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        diphoton_events = photons[\n",
    "            has_two_photons\n",
    "        ]\n",
    "\n",
    "        diphoton_mass = (\n",
    "            diphoton_events[:, 0]\n",
    "            + diphoton_events[:, 1]\n",
    "        ).M\n",
    "\n",
    "        diphoton_mass_list.append(\n",
    "            diphoton_mass\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Electron-photon reconstruction\n",
    "        has_electron_and_photon = (\n",
    "            (ak.num(electrons, axis=1) >= 1)\n",
    "            & (ak.num(photons, axis=1) >= 1)\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        electron_photon_electrons = electrons[\n",
    "            has_electron_and_photon\n",
    "        ]\n",
    "\n",
    "        electron_photon_photons = photons[\n",
    "            has_electron_and_photon\n",
    "        ]\n",
    "\n",
    "        electron_photon_mass = (\n",
    "            electron_photon_electrons[:, 0]\n",
    "            + electron_photon_photons[:, 0]\n",
    "        ).M\n",
    "\n",
    "        electron_photon_mass_list.append(\n",
    "            electron_photon_mass\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Finished processing the sample.\")"
   ]
  },
