    "### Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "Here we see that while greatly outnumbered by matched candidates, unmatched leptons are still a significant factor in our data, even including our cuts and matching criteria. This not only demonstrates the presence of misidentified lepton candidates, but further shows that non-prompts are accounted for in the truth record, showing a clear distinction in nature between misidentified and non-prompt fakes. \n",
    "\n",
    "Why are there so many more objects misidentified as electrons than muons? The most direct answer to this has to do with the masses involved and the differences in mass between the two. This difference directly influences the signatures that these particles leave in our detectors. \n",
    "The electron deposits most of its energy as it enters the detector material and is usually fully stopped by the electromagnetic calorimeter.  Particles in hadronic jets can often mimic this signature and be picked up as an electron, even if the source of the signal came from a non-lepton source. Muons, on the other hand, are recognized by signatures in the outer muon spectrometer. For something to be misidentified as a muon, it must first pass through the calorimeter array and leave a trail in the muon spectrometer with enough energy to be recognized, which is a far less likely pheonomenon. \n",
    "\n",
    "While these numbers do indicate the presence of fake leptons via misidentification, we cannot assume these numbers are completely synonymous. As mentioned before, a significant amount of these unmatched candidates may also have arisen from other factors, like the resolution of the detector for this run, or the strictness of our matching requirements. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8741e9ba",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Real Data and Mitigation\n",
    "\n",
    "In a formal analysis, both finding and filtering fake leptons is a far greater task than what was shown here. Without prior knowledge of what is contained in a sample, researchers are forced to use data-driven methods, rather than simple reconstruction and filtering, to identify fakes. Utilizing sample weights and the real/fake 'efficiency' fractions, analysts use methods such as the fake-factor and matrix techniques across multiple samples to study fake lepton contributions. If you have any interest in performing your own detector-data analysis, you can refer to our most recent publication on [tools for estimating fake/non-prompt lepton backgrounds](https://cds.cern.ch/record/2842463/files/2211.16178.pdf) for a deep dive on fake leptons in ATLAS data!"
   ]
